      "text": "To solve the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\), we first need to complete the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nStarting with the given equation:\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\]\n\nRearrange the terms to bring all terms to one side:\n\\[x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for the \\(x\\) terms and the \\(y\\) terms. For the \\(x\\) terms, \\(x^2 - 10x\\), we take half of \\(-10\\), which is \\(-5\\), and then square it to get \\(25\\). For the \\(y\\) terms, \\(y^2 + 6y\\), we take half of \\(6\\), which is \\(3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\). Adding and subtracting these squares within the equation, we get:\n\\[x^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = -34 + 25 + 9\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = 5\\]\n\\[y + 3 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = -3\\]\n\nSo, the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are \\(x = 5\\) and \\(y = -3\\). Therefore, the sum \\(x + y\\) is:\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}\\]",
